    "narrative": "The lineup for LawDog today centers on the elite production expected from **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (81.68) and **Cal Raleigh** (75.22). **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** is our highest-rated play, buoyed significantly by a strong 40-PA BvP historical edge against Singer and a favorable spot in the two-hole, while **Cal Raleigh** benefits from a massive platoon advantage against Lauer, whose 5.30 xERA offers a prime environment for power output. **Daulton Varsho** also cracks the top tier (69.94) thanks to a favorable matchup against Littell and a dynamic platoon advantage that boosts his efficiency well above his baseline projection, making him a critical component of today's scoring core.\n\nThe optimizer faced a tight decision at the fringe of the starting rotation, ultimately relegating **Gavin Sheets** (46.29) to the bench. While **Gavin Sheets** holds a positive platoon edge against Hughes, he lacks the multi-category upside required to displace starters like **Tyler Freeman** or **Lawrence Butler**, whose park factors and positional advantages provide a higher, albeit volatile, ceiling. We are keeping a close eye on the weather and the wind out at the park, which adds some upside to the scores of **Geraldo Perdomo**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, and **Luis Arraez**, potentially pushing them toward the 60-point \"Strong\" threshold if the ball begins carrying early.\n\nStrategic risks remain concentrated on **Taylor Ward**, who draws the toughest matchup of the day against Skubal. Despite an elite 2.62 xERA for the opposing pitcher, **Taylor Ward** remains in the lineup due to our premium on leadoff hitters and a decent platoon bump, but his 53.15 score is vulnerable to an early exit if Skubal dominates. We have successfully avoided deploying **Jeremiah Jackson** and **Braden Montgomery**, both of whom fell below our 40-point efficiency floor due to severe SP skill headwinds, ensuring we preserve our game caps for players with higher daily win probability.",
